course: dictionary
lesson: 11475
title: Words from far away
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Afar means far away, at a great distance. It is an old-fashioned word, so you will meet it mostly in stories, poems and songs. You will most often see it in the phrase from afar.
explain: «Travellers came from afar to see the king's new castle.» The clue is came: they journeyed a long way to get there. So afar means from a long way away.
explain: «She admired him from afar, and never spoke to him.» Here from afar means from a distance. She watched him and admired him, but she did not go close or talk to him.
explain: Afar is almost always used in phrases. «People came to the fair from near and afar» means people came from places both close and far away. Try the swap test: put far away into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Afar means far away, at a great distance.
fact: Afar is old-fashioned, so you will find it mostly in stories, poems and songs.
fact: The phrase «from afar» means from a long way away.
fact: «She admired him from afar» means she admired him from a distance, without talking to him.
fact: The phrase «near and afar» means in places both close and far away.
exercise: «Travellers came from afar to see the castle.» What does from afar mean? | *From a long way away | Very quickly | From under the bed | From the next street
exercise: «She admired him from afar.» What does this tell you? | She shouted at him across the room | She gave him a present and a hug | *She watched him from a distance and did not talk to him | She ran to meet him at the door
exercise: Afar is an old-fashioned word. Where would you most likely find it? | In a maths sum about adding numbers | *In a story or a poem about a faraway land | In a text message to a friend | On a shopping list
test: «People came to the fair from near and afar.» What does near and afar mean? | *In places both close and far away | In the same place every year | Under the big tent | Only in the town square
test: Which phrase means from a long way away? | from behind | from inside | from above | *from afar
exam: «The sailor longed to see his home, which lay afar across the sea.» What does afar mean in this sentence? | Happy and proud | *Far away, at a great distance | Under the sea | Very close by
